WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.709
A processor asks memory for a number.

2
00:00:02.709 --> 00:00:05.739
Why can it not just take the answer immediately?

3
00:00:05.739 --> 00:00:12.304
Let us slow one read down, and follow two numbers that look similar but mean very different things.

4
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.427
The first number is sixteen.

5
00:00:14.427 --> 00:00:18.053
Think of it as a location, like a numbered slot.

6
00:00:18.053 --> 00:00:22.149
The second number is forty two: the value stored in that slot.

7
00:00:22.149 --> 00:00:23.984
An address answers where.

8
00:00:23.984 --> 00:00:26.181
The stored value answers what.

9
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:29.457
The processor puts sixteen on the address lines.

10
00:00:29.457 --> 00:00:32.593
Follow that selection into the memory table.

11
00:00:32.593 --> 00:00:36.196
It picks one row, and that row contains forty two.

12
00:00:36.196 --> 00:00:42.211
Changing the address would select a different row, not change the number inside this row.

13
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.787
But selecting a row and receiving its contents are separate events.

14
00:00:46.787 --> 00:00:53.974
In our teaching model, the data lines still show an old value, nineteen, while memory prepares the answer.

15
00:00:53.974 --> 00:00:55.894
Those lines carry a number.

16
00:00:55.894 --> 00:00:58.902
That alone does not make it the right number.

17
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.856
Imagine sampling now.

18
00:01:00.856 --> 00:01:07.937
The processor would capture nineteen, even though we asked for location sixteen and expected forty two.

19
00:01:07.937 --> 00:01:10.305
The mistake is not arithmetic.

20
00:01:10.305 --> 00:01:14.912
We have confused a value that is present with a value that is ready.

21
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.013
Now memory presents forty two and indicates that the data is valid.

22
00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:25.475
Under this model's rules, the processor samples only after that indication.

23
00:01:25.475 --> 00:01:27.779
The address stays sixteen throughout.

24
00:01:27.779 --> 00:01:31.754
What changes is our permission to trust the returned data.

25
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:35.365
We can draw the same story as a timing diagram.

26
00:01:35.365 --> 00:01:37.520
A read request comes first.

27
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.589
Data becomes valid later.

28
00:01:39.589 --> 00:01:42.768
Sampling occurs inside that valid interval.

29
00:01:42.768 --> 00:01:50.737
The traces share one time axis; they are three views of the same transaction, not three unrelated pictures.

30
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:53.116
So keep these questions separate.

31
00:01:53.116 --> 00:01:54.801
Where should memory look?

32
00:01:54.801 --> 00:01:56.337
That is the address.

33
00:01:56.337 --> 00:01:59.068
When can the processor use the answer?

34
00:01:59.068 --> 00:02:01.095
That is the validity rule.

35
00:02:01.095 --> 00:02:06.528
The important idea is an agreement about when information becomes trustworthy.
